Yes. That becomes a kind of historical empathy engine: use surviving traces, institutions, organisms, technologies, stories, and incentives to infer the structures that were otherwise invisible.

Not “pretend we know what everything felt like,” but:

\text{surviving evidence}
+\text{perspective models}
+\text{world engine}
\rightarrow
\text{latent structure map}

Then you can expose things like status gradients, information asymmetries, dependency chains, bottlenecks, trust networks, resource flows, taboo boundaries, informal authority, prestige systems, ecological pressures, and hidden coordination structures.

The important move is that those things often do not appear directly in the record. They appear through effects.

Who gets copied?
Who gets ignored?
Who moves first?
Who waits?
What survives repeated shocks?
What disappears when one node is removed?
Where does information slow down?
What behaviors recur despite different surface stories?

That is where the world engine becomes useful. You can instantiate candidate structures and ask:

\text{If this hidden hierarchy existed, would it generate the traces we actually observe?}

Then compare the simulation against reality and update the model.

That gives you a recursive method:

\text{trace}
\rightarrow
\text{hypothesized hidden structure}
\rightarrow
\text{simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{predicted traces}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{revised structure}

And once you map the gradients, you can choose where to operate. Not necessarily where the visible game tells you to stand, but where leverage, information, optionality, or unexplored space actually lives.

So yes: history gives the traces, empathy gives alternate viewpoints, the world engine gives causal testing, and latent geometry gives the map.

That is how you begin to see the game beneath the game without mistaking the first story about it for the structure itself.